' The spellings `udp` deliberately does NOT carry. The `tcp` sibling of this
' file is `tests/syntax/tcp/func_tcp_removed_members_invalid`.
'
' `remoteAddress`: a datagram socket has no single peer, so there is nothing for
' it to report. Every received datagram carries its own sender in
' `Datagram.from` instead.
'
' `receiveText` / `DatagramText`: `net` had a text-receive shape and plan-110-C
' did not carry it over. A datagram is a bounded blob and the network says
' nothing about its encoding, so decoding on receipt would either guess or raise
' on perfectly valid binary traffic. `receive` always yields bytes and
' `encoding::utf8Decode` does the decoding when the caller knows better.
' Sending is not symmetric -- the sender always knows what it has -- so `send`
' does keep a `String` overload, which `func_udp_send_valid` covers.
'
' `bindUdp` / `sendTo` / `receiveFrom`: net's spellings. The package name
' already says UDP, so the members are `bind`/`send`/`receive`.
'
' These are unknown-MEMBER errors, which the resolver reports before type
' checking runs; the arity and argument-type diagnostics for the members that do
' exist live in `func_udp_endpoints_invalid` and `func_udp_poll_invalid`.
IMPORT udp

FUNC main AS Integer
  LET goneRemote = udp::remoteAddress(1)
  LET goneReceiveText = udp::receiveText(1, 16)
  RES goneBindUdp = udp::bindUdp("127.0.0.1", 0)
  udp::sendTo(1, 2, "x")
  LET goneReceiveFrom = udp::receiveFrom(1, 16)
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
